Let F_2 denote the binary field. Fix a positive integer n and a polynomial f(x) = x^n − a_{n−1}x^{n−1} − ⋯ − a_1 x − a_0 in F_2[x] with a_0 ≠ 0. The polycyclic code associated with f is, by definition, an ideal of the quotient ring F_2[x]/⟨f(x)⟩, identified with a linear subspace C ⊆ F_2^n via the standard coefficient correspondence. Equip F_2^n with the usual Euclidean inner product ⟨u,v⟩ = Σ u_i v_i, and let C^⊥ = { v ∈ F_2^n : ⟨u,v⟩ = 0 for all u ∈ C } denote the dual code. The code C is called:
 • self-dual if C = C^⊥;
 • self-orthogonal if C ⊆ C^⊥;
 • dual-containing if C^⊥ ⊆ C.

Question: Does there exist any choice of n ≥ 1 and of f(x) ∈ F_2[x] (monic, with nonzero constant term) such that the associated polycyclic code C ⊆ F_2^n is self-dual, self-orthogonal, or dual-containing? Equivalently, prove or disprove the statement that no nontrivial polycyclic code over F_2 can be self-dual, self-orthogonal, or dual-containing.

